<h1>Help</h1>
<p>
  Each row of the list is a <strong>key</strong> of OpenCutList's interface: on the left the reference text (the French
  source, or the English translation if you pick it above the column), on the right your translation. Write it, then
  save: it is saved without leaving the page.
</p>

<h2>Statuses</h2>
<dl>
  <dt class="flex h-6 items-center gap-2 self-start"><%= status_dot "outdated" %> <strong>To review</strong></dt><dd>The French text changed since your translation. Check it, fix it if needed, then save, even unchanged, to validate it again.</dd>
  <dt class="flex h-6 items-center gap-2 self-start"><%= status_dot "not_translated" %> <strong>Not translated</strong></dt><dd>Nothing written in your language yet.</dd>
  <dt class="flex h-6 items-center gap-2 self-start"><%= status_dot "not_approved" %> <strong>Not approved</strong></dt><dd>Translated, not reviewed yet.</dd>
  <dt class="flex h-6 items-center gap-2 self-start"><%= status_dot "approved" %> <strong>Approved</strong></dt><dd>Validated by a reviewer of the language.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Reviewers have an <strong>Approve</strong> button next to <strong>Save</strong>.</p>

<h2>The editor</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    Some parts of the French text must be found as is in the translation: variables <code>{{ count }}</code>, references
    to other keys <code>$t(tab.cutlist.title)</code>, HTML tags <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> and entities
    <code>&amp;nbsp;</code>. They are <strong>locked</strong>: you can move them within the sentence, not change them. When
    one is missing, a chip under the editor puts it back; an unknown one shows in red.
  </li>
  <li><kbd>⌘</kbd>-click (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>-click) on a <code>$t(…)</code> reference opens the key it points to in a new tab.</li>
  <li>
    Keep the Markdown: <code>**bold**</code>, <code>*italic*</code>, links <code>[text](url)</code>. Translate the link's
    text, keep its url.
  </li>
  <li>
    The <strong>proposals</strong> under the editor (🤖 machine translation, "Same text elsewhere") fill it in one click:
    read it again before saving.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Warnings</strong> ⚠ (final dot, spaces, line count…) don't prevent saving: they point at what deserves a
    look.
  </li>
  <li>Greyed keys are <strong>copied</strong> as is into every language: nothing to translate.</li>
  <li>
    Above the list, <kbd>¶</kbd> shows spaces and line breaks, the key button always shows the keys' names (else on
    hover).
  </li>
  <li>An edited translation not saved yet has a blue background; the page warns you before leaving if any are left.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Shortcuts</h2>
<dl>
  <dt><kbd>⌘/Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd></dt><dd>Save</dd>
  <dt><kbd>⌘/Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd></dt><dd>Save and approve (reviewers)</dd>
  <dt><kbd>⌘/Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>F</kbd></dt><dd>Go to the search (a 2nd time: the browser's)</dd>
  <dt><kbd>Esc</kbd></dt><dd>Leave the search field</dd>
  <dt><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>Esc</kbd></dt><dd>Clear the search, filters and sort</dd>
</dl>

<h2>Search and filters</h2>
<p>
  The search looks into the texts. The <strong>Filters</strong> menu narrows the list by status, to open questions,
  warnings or recently changed keys; the <strong>Sort</strong> menu orders it by modification date. In the search field,
  before <kbd>Enter</kbd>:
</p>
<dl>
  <dt><code>#120</code>, <code>#120-180</code></dt><dd>by row number</dd>
  <dt><code>key:…</code></dt><dd>by key name</dd>
  <dt><code>branch:…</code></dt><dd>by branch (<code>tab.cutlist</code>)</dd>
  <dt><code>user:…</code></dt><dd>by author (name or its start)</dd>
</dl>

<h2>Asking a question</h2>
<p>
  Unsure what a text means? The <strong>comments</strong> button under each translation lets you ask a question about
  that key: the admins and your language's reviewers are told, and the answer shows in the same place. For anything else
  (access, a new language, a problem with the tool…), write to us with the form.
</p>
